.Dd November 16, 2026
.Dt SND_ACP 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm snd_acp
.Nd "AMD Audio Coprocessor digital microphone driver"
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile this driver into the kernel, place the following lines in your
kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "device sound"
.Cd "device snd_acp"
.Ed
.Pp
Alternatively, to load the driver as a module at boot time, place the
following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
snd_acp_load="YES"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver allows the generic audio driver,
.Xr sound 4 ,
to record from the built-in digital microphones connected to the PDM
interface of the AMD Audio Coprocessor (ACP) found in recent Ryzen
processors.
It provides one recording channel, stereo, 32-bit signed little-endian
samples at 48 kHz; the
.Xr sound 4
feeders convert to other formats and rates.
.Pp
The driver attaches only when the firmware reports digital microphones:
the ACP pin configuration must route the PDM interface, and the ACPI
.Va _WOV
method of the ACP device, when present, must not return 0.
The ACP's DSP and its I2S and SoundWire links are not used.
.Sh HARDWARE
The
.Nm
driver supports the following devices:
.Pp
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
AMD Audio Coprocessor 7.x (PCI 1022:15e2, revisions 0x70 to 0x72),
as found in the Ryzen AI Max (Strix Halo) processors
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sound 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Fx 16.0 .
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Olivier Cochard Aq Mt olivier@FreeBSD.org
.Sh BUGS
The microphones' gain cannot be changed; there is no mixer.
Recordings carry a DC offset that the driver does not remove.
